gaps.what_readers_meet:
Runtime, syscall, time, math and crypto are full of bodyless declarations. Examples: `//go:linkname newTimer` followed by `internal static partial ж<Timer> newTimer(...);` (time/sleep.cs:43-50), and `internal static partial float64 archMax(float64 x, float64 y);` (math/dim_asm.cs:11). The summary never states the base rule. A bodyless Go function becomes a C# `partial` method, completed in one of these ways:
- a linkname pull or push forwarder;
- a forwarder for a pure-JMP assembly trampoline;
- a purego body;
- a hand-owned `_impl.cs`;
- PartialStubGenerator's throwing stub.
The summary also does not say how a reader tells these apart. The pieces sit in three summary sections, and linkname appears only as link text.
